    "evidence_text": "Reconstitution of the immune system after hematopoietic stem cell transplantation in humans\n\nHematopoietic stem cell transplantation is associated with a severe immune deficiency.\n\nInnate immunity, including epithelial barriers, monocytes, granulocytes, and NK cells recovers within weeks after transplantation.\n\nB- and T-cell counts normalize during the first months after transplantation, but in particular, T-cell immunity may remain impaired for years.\n\nTCR stimulation with modified anti-CD3 mAb expands CD8+ T cell population and induces CD8+CD25+ Tregs.\n\nUCB transplantation has traditionally been associated with delayed engraftment, poor immune reconstitution and consequent increased risk of infection.\n\nMore recent clinical studies, however, suggest that conditioning regimens and in particular the omission of in vivo T-cell depletion may play a crucial role in post-transplant T-cell expansion, facilitating a uniquely rapid immune recovery after UCB transplantation.\n\nAfter 6 months, cord blood-graft recipients approximated the TCR diversity of healthy individuals, whereas recipients of T cell-depleted peripheral-blood stem cell grafts had 28-fold and 14-fold lower CD4(+) and CD8(+) T cell diversities, respectively.",
